5417] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429726 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1033 990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1034 0.7291506299743056 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050001 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "25757680-24c4-582e-828e-48263c608c8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428229 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428230 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428231 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428232 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428233 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving 3x3 Singular Systems of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1033 1374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1034 0.7261066881298396 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050178 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "340f0f9d-19b0-5cff-abb5-75d2e7e75aa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186436961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186436961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122714 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ca34d82-cd28-5172-85b5-f4d4887562de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124411 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124411 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the matrix\n![](images/af/af05cedf7b2015f4f61df71e833390aa845d50126c3203369320ac6feb8b2cc3.svg)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124411 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124412 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124412 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/6e/6e51c22c33c32d52c51657552a3c174d706a316ec10c78a4a953d2fcebc304ff.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124412 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/0e/0e25fe1b46b828e6fac168d1755af7aed9a7c3f657d1a6d6677fd71746b7ce31.svg) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/54/54dad1c9d747fead6f27dd0e9af7cca434c40a83d56c02c854bb610a1cdc6087.svg)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/d2/d28cf572126716928bc9ded7c2646871540e20b1d93d1efc929599b4c379d38b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124413 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124413 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the matrix \n![](images/3d/3da867d461c2b42c21badd67ce59c9ec5f6448597b4d2dcbb7e8b28ffe397768.svg) \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124413 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/04/04f6efaa2bb141940040a04905e52aacd7aa739e1bd58dd71666a10543ff0f74.svg)\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/dd/dd78cbf2025fdf012dc27429596c84be09825b436c32a5410f50f78cd4943a66.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/5b/5b5b06d4cd243ca0370807b58bc9d0da68f34aa008956016bab3da29377df219.svg)\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  ![](images/3e/3ea698aa474cb6d28b3fb511215fbe5da7d01b6653e884719d99483eac5635a0.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/b7/b736228d68ad58b430f707236014be76e0354bf42ff9fcd73c495a077835bad7.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124414 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8575" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124414 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/4e/4e737b78109f94302bdc256b4a8bb9585fdb214ccc89bd9439b52fa4973f59ba.svg) You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n![](images/6b/6b4045bf6fc71339953a55a1e3660384a569861537ee37427be110aa3442a872.svg) ![](images/34/34760505c150775e9b579151b333cf3baa044102e0197b4e9aae3444c0581cd0.svg) are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124414 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/42/427a788b1c9f78e8ce25f06b54cef549d7be04757f6275cc66f12bd48652e40b.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/bd/bdf233e22e7972a4eb912463bc81727d7e22b160ff823ec51d0be494d24d493c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124599 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124599 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124599 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124599 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [